<Guide lang="en" slug="finca-apartment-or-village-house-families" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="aankoopkeuring" ctaTitle="Found a home for the whole family?" ctaText="We survey it before you sign and tell you honestly what needs doing before the children can live there safely and comfortably.">
  <h2 id="core">What really matters</h2>
  <p>A family that emigrates chooses not just a house but a daily routine. How do the children get to school? Can they walk to a friend’s house or to football? How many hours a day will you spend in the car? Those questions matter more than the view or the square metres. For the paperwork of the move itself, see our guide to <a href="/en/guides/moving-to-spain-from-the-uk/">moving to Spain</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="school">School and school transport</h2>
  <p>Most villages in the Axarquía have a state primary school (<em>CEIP</em>). Secondary schools (<em>IES</em>) are in the larger towns, such as Vélez-Málaga, Torre del Mar, Nerja, Torrox and Cómpeta. Children in rural areas often get free school transport, but the bus does not stop at every finca. If you live a few kilometres down a dirt track, you will drive the children to a pick-up point or to school every day.</p>
  <p>International schools are mainly towards Málaga and the western Costa del Sol. If that is your choice, living on the coast or near the motorway is more practical than high in the mountains.</p>

  <h2 id="social">Friends and independence</h2>
  <p>In a village or on the coast, children soon walk to school, the square or the sports club on their own. That is good for their Spanish and their independence, and for your diary. On a finca they depend on you for every play date. For young children that is fine; for teenagers who want to go out or find a part-time job, often not.</p>

  <h2 id="space">Space and outdoor life</h2>
  <p>This is where the finca wins. Your own land, a pool, room for a vegetable garden, a dog or chickens: for many families that is exactly why they move to Spain. An apartment usually has a shared pool and a terrace, a village house a roof terrace and the village square. A middle way is a house on the edge of a village, or a villa on urban land with a small garden.</p>
  <p>On a finca, mind safety: an unfenced pool, steep terraces and open irrigation tanks (<em>albercas</em>) are risks with small children.</p>

  <h2 id="work">Work and internet</h2>
  <p>If you work remotely, reliable internet is a must. On the coast and in many villages there is fibre. On a finca you often rely on 4G/5G or satellite; test the signal on site, at the time you would normally be on calls. If you work outside the home, count how many car journeys the family makes per day. With two working parents and school-age children, two cars on a finca are almost unavoidable.</p>

  <h2 id="costs">Costs and renovation</h2>
  <p>Families need space, which makes a finca or a large village house attractive on price per square metre. But adding a bedroom to a finca with AFO status is usually not allowed. So look at what legally stands there now, not at what you want to make of it later. A village house on urban land offers more scope, for example an extra bedroom or bathroom within the existing volume.</p>
  <p>Want the house ready when the children start school? Plan the <a href="/en/renovation/">renovation</a> with time to spare: permits and works in the Axarquía easily take a few months. See also <a href="/en/guides/renovation-costs-spain/">renovation costs</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="advice">Our advice by type</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Apartment</strong>: a good first step. Rent or buy on the coast for a year, get to know the area and only then choose your permanent spot. Less suited if you want lots of space and outdoor life.</li>
    <li><strong>Village house</strong>: often the best choice for families with school-age children. School, friends and sport within walking distance, and you settle into village life quickly. Watch the stairs and outdoor space.</li>
    <li><strong>Finca</strong>: ideal for families with young children who want space and nature and accept the driving and upkeep. Choose one close to a village with a school, on a passable road.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Have a <a href="/en/property-survey/">property survey</a> done before you sign. Then you know what needs doing before the family can live there safely and comfortably, and what it costs.</p>
</Guide>
